  "result": "```js\nasync function getUser(id) {\n  const rows = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id]);\n  if (!rows.length) throw new Error('Not found');\n  return rows[0];\n}\n```\n\nKey changes:\n- `async` replaces the `callback` parameter\n- `await` replaces the callback's `err`/`result` split \u2014 errors from `db.query` propagate automatically as thrown exceptions\n- Early `throw` replaces `callback(new Error(...))`, and `return rows[0]` replaces `callback(null, rows[0])`\n\nCallers use it with `try/catch` or `.catch()`:\n\n```js\n// async context\ntry {\n  const user = await getUser(id);\n} catch (err) {\n  console.error(err);\n}\n\n// or promise chain\ngetUser(id).then(user => ...).catch(err => ...);\n```\n\nNote: this assumes `db.query` already returns a Promise. If it's still callback-based, wrap it first with `util.promisify(db.query.bind(db))`.",
